module
public import CFSG.Quasithin.Chapter15.Section03.CharacteristicTwo.RankOneConfiguration.Recognition.SmallExtension.MarkedMS.AlternatingSixReconstruction.Exceptional.Exhaustion.LargeDistance.CubicGeneratingShifts
public import CFSG.Quasithin.Chapter15.Section03.CharacteristicTwo.RankOneConfiguration.Recognition.SmallExtension.MarkedMS.AlternatingSixReconstruction.Natural.Exhaustion.LargeDistance.SectionFive.TerminalLine

/-!
# The cubic full-point terminal bound with both central covers

The whole initial moved subgroup is already marked as the natural four-space
or the augmentation five-space. In either cover, the unique transposition
of a point core has a noncentral displacement fixed by that entire point
core. The model calculation is shared with the natural branch, but is applied
here to the original cubic projection and its whole moving subgroup.

At a critical pair, a small image is generated by a transposition; a large
quadratic image contains that same transposition. Transport through the
literal factor embedding gives a noncentral element of the critical
commutator in the first edge seed. Two generating backward shifts would
centralize this subgroup at two preceding vertices. The same component
transporter fixes it pointwise and brings the second centrality back to its
critical start, a contradiction.

Thus full point-core equality implies critical distance at most four on
the original cubic graph, retaining its odd kernel and both cover orders.
The whole-core and literal C.1.32 consumers reduce this branch to distance
four. Full point-core equality, the other radical images, the distance-four
exclusion, natural exhaustion and both final reconstructions remain open.

Source: Meierfrankenfeld--Stellmacher (1993), (3.3)(ii), (5.10)--(5.12),
printed pp.866--867, 921--927. The terminal graph argument adapts the checked
Natural.Exhaustion.LargeDistance.SectionFive.TerminalLine proof without
identifying its replacement graph or kernel with the cubic ones.
-/

